<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBA"><a href="./File:LycaenidJavadi1.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBQ"><img resource="./File:LycaenidJavadi1.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/LycaenidJavadi1.jpg/250px-LycaenidJavadi1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/LycaenidJavadi1.jpg/500px-LycaenidJavadi1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="950" data-file-height="908" data-file-type="bitmap" height="239" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBg"/></a><figcaption id="mwBw"><i id="mwCA">Rapala</i> species from India</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCQ"><i id="mwCg"><b id="mwCw">Rapala</b></i> is a genus of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Butterflies" title="Butterflies" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDA">butterflies</a> in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tribe_(biology)" title="Tribe (biology)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDQ">tribe</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Deudorigini" title="Deudorigini" id="mwDg">Deudorigini</a> of the subfamily <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Theclinae" title="Theclinae" id="mwDw">Theclinae</a> of the family <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Lycaenidae" title="Lycaenidae" id="mwEA">Lycaenidae</a>. They are found throughout <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./South_Asia" title="South Asia" id="mwEQ">South Asia</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Southeast_Asia" title="Southeast Asia" id="mwEg">Southeast Asia</a>, with a few species extending to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Australia" title="Australia" id="mwEw">Australia</a> and into the eastern <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Palaearctic" title="Palaearctic" class="mw-redirect" id="mwFA">Palaearctic</a> region.</p>

<p id="mwFQ">Males of <i id="mwFg">Rapala</i> are differentiated from other <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Genera" title="Genera" class="mw-redirect" id="mwFw">genera</a> in the Deudorigini by their <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Lepidoptera_genitalia" title="Lepidoptera genitalia" id="mwGA">genitalia</a>, with the conjoined <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Valvae" title="Valvae" class="mw-redirect" id="mwGQ">valvae</a> tapering evenly to rounded apices. The male secondary sexual characters also differ. In every species there is a brand above the origin of vein seven and lying wholly within space seven, clothed with very small scent scales, and nearly always there is an associated erectile hair tuft on the forewing dorsum beneath.</p><!-- do add the reference here -->

<p id="mwGg">Almost all species exhibit <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sexual_dimorphism" title="Sexual dimorphism" id="mwGw">sexual dimorphism</a> in the colour of the upperside; normally the male is red, reddish brown or deep blue, while the female is dark brown or pale purple blue.<sup about="#mwt4" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-1"}}'><a href="./Rapala_(butterfly)#cite_note-1" id="mwHA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwHQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHg">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

